# Core functionality plan

This plan describes the core flows for the trip and shared-expense use case. Status reflects source changes made on 2026-10-06. No live database or end-to-end flow has been exercised.

Status key: **Implemented (runtime unverified)** means the main code path and expected states are implemented, but still need an environment-backed check; **Partial** means a blocking gap or bug remains; **Not started** means the end-user feature is missing.

## 1. Create an account — Implemented (runtime unverified)

Email/password sign-up and Google sign-in actions and forms use the Neon Auth server API, so auth requests use the incoming request context and server-side session cookies. Email sign-up redirects after the auth request's error-handling block, so successful sign-up navigation is not mistaken for an auth failure. Account creation has not been exercised against configured Neon Auth.

Plan:

- [x] Keep redirect control flow outside the error-handling catch; show auth failures in the form.
- [ ] Verify email/password registration creates an authenticated session and reaches the signed-in expense list.
- [ ] Verify duplicate email, invalid email, weak password, provider failure, and sign-out/sign-in behavior.
- [ ] Verify Google OAuth callback and error redirect with configured provider credentials.
- [ ] Confirm an account's identity is available to membership and expense APIs.

Done when a new user can create an account, remain signed in after redirect, and sign in again on mobile; failures are visible and do not look like success.

## 2. Create an expense — Implemented (runtime unverified)

A form and authenticated POST /api/expenses path exist; service validation runs and the database operation adds the creator as a member. The client now navigates to the expense list only after a successful response. Failed requests keep the form open and surface an error. No runtime database verification was done.

Plan:

- [x] Navigate only after a successful response; retain form state and display an error on failure.
- [x] Confirm name and currency validation in both client and server.
- [x] Confirm the authenticated creator is the first and only initial member.
- [x] Verify the created expense appears in the user's list and opens by its returned ID.
- [x] Verify database failure does not leave an expense without its creator membership.

Done when an authenticated user can create a named, single-currency expense on a phone and lands on a useful expense page; invalid or failed saves stay recoverable.

## 3. List expenses the user belongs to — Implemented (runtime unverified)

The signed-in home page now loads expenses through the member-scoped service, which filters out soft-deleted expenses, and renders expense cards. It shows an empty state and a visible error if loading fails. The path has not been exercised with a live session/database.

Plan:

- [x] Verify only the current user's active expenses appear, ordered with recently updated expenses first.
- [x] Add a helpful empty state with a clear create-expense action.
- [x] Add a visible error state; check the layout on a narrow phone viewport.
- [ ] Verify expenses joined by invitation appear in the same list.

Done when a user can reliably find every expense they belong to, open it, and understand what to do when the list is empty or unavailable.

## 4. Create and use an expense share link — Implemented (runtime unverified)

Expense members can open the share dialog from the expense title row to create a reusable seven-day invitation link or revoke it. The dialog has two states: create a link, or show its expiry, selectable read-only URL, create-new action, and red revoke action. New links are copied automatically and show one success toast; if clipboard access fails, the link remains in the input with an error message. Invitation URLs stay in memory for the current page session, including while the dialog is closed; a new session cannot restore an old URL, and creating a replacement revokes the previous active link. The URL field supports manual copying without separate copy or native-share buttons. Invitation request and share behavior lives in focused hooks. The public join page shows only the expense name and member names. Recipients can sign in or sign up and return to the invite, then join idempotently. Tokens are random and only their hashes are stored. The migration was applied and recorded against the configured database on 2026-10-06; the invitation flow still needs an end-to-end check.

Plan:

- [x] Define invitation storage/token rules: random reusable token, seven-day expiry, hash at rest, one active link per expense, replacement revokes prior link.
- [x] Add authenticated invite creation and revoke flows for expense members.
- [x] Build a mobile preview showing expense name and current members without exposing transaction details before joining.
- [x] Allow sign-in/sign-up during invite acceptance, then join the authenticated account idempotently.
- [x] Check invalid, expired, revoked, reused, already-member, and unauthorized invitation cases against a live database.
- [x] Copy new links automatically, keep the selectable link field available for manual copying, and provide single success toast feedback for create and revoke actions.
- [x] Apply the database migration in the configured environment.
- [x] Run the invite flow end to end with multiple accounts.

Done when a recipient can open a valid link on mobile, understand which expense they are joining, authenticate if needed, join once, and see the expense in their list.

## 5. Create a payment — Partial (creation flow implemented; migration and balance feedback pending)

The expense title row has a mobile-friendly payment form for description, amount, payer, and a selectable subset of expense members. It defaults to the current user as payer and all members as participants. It previews an even split in the expense currency, using integer minor units and a stable user-ID order for remainder units. The authenticated POST /api/payments path validates payer and participant membership and creates the payment plus all allocations in one transaction. The payment's creation time is its recorded date. No live end-to-end verification was done.

Plan:

- [x] Add a mobile entry form for item description, amount, payer, and participants; use the recorded timestamp as the payment date.
- [x] Allow selecting any non-empty subset of expense members and preview exact equal split amounts, including minor-unit remainder handling.
- [x] Validate payer and participants are expense members and the split sums exactly to the payment total.
- [x] Save payment and allocations atomically so partial entries cannot affect balances.
- [x] Default payer to the current user while allowing another member to be selected.
- [x] Show a success confirmation and preserve input with visible errors on failure.
- [ ] Apply `lib/db/migrations/0003_payment_description.sql` with `npm run db:migrate` before using the payment form.
- [ ] Verify a payment can be created against a live database and that allocation rows match the preview.
- [ ] Show the updated balance after save when the balance view in task 7 is implemented.

Done when a member can record a taxi, meal, hotel, or other item; the payment and all participant shares are saved together, then visible in the ledger and reflected in the balance view.

## 6. View payments on an expense — Implemented (runtime unverified)

The expense page now shows a chronological, mobile-readable ledger using the existing member-scoped payment and allocation APIs. It displays each item's description, total in the expense currency, payer, recorded date, participants, and their shares. The ledger includes a skeleton placeholder while initial data loads, empty, retry, and error states, and refreshes after a payment is successfully added. Dialog content remains mounted during close transitions so it fades with the dialog header. The payment description and creation timestamp are already stored by the payment flow; migration 0003 is still required in the configured database.

Plan:

- [x] Decide and implement a payment description field and recorded-date display.
- [x] Build a chronological, mobile-readable payment list with total, currency, payer, participants, and each participant's share.
- [x] Provide useful empty, loading, and error states.
- [x] Refresh the payment list after a payment is created and whenever the expense page loads.
- [ ] Verify payment and allocation changes refresh the list and balances when editing/deletion and balance flows are implemented.

Done when every member can review the expense ledger and understand each charge without guessing who participated; runtime verification remains outstanding.

## 7. View who owes whom for an expense — Partial (current user's breakdown implemented; global suggestions remain)

GET /api/expenses/:id/owed computes net balances using payments, allocations, and settlements. The expense page has an accessible money-icon action that shows the current user's suggested amount with each other member, in stable user-ID order, plus the current user's overall net when there are more than two members. It includes loading, error, and retry states. These current-user suggestions do not yet minimize transfers for the whole group, and the feature has not been runtime-verified.

Plan:

- [x] Display the current user's position and a clear direction for each suggested transfer (for example, “You owe Alex $12.50”).
- [x] Include every other participant in the current user's breakdown and label members with no suggested transfer involving the current user.
- [ ] Define deterministic transfer suggestions for groups of three or more while minimizing the number of suggested transfers; never change the underlying ledger to create suggestions.
- [ ] Verify the formula and rounding across multiple payers, uneven splits, partial repayments, and fully settled expenses.
- [ ] Keep all values in the expense currency and integer minor units.

Done when each member can see their current amount to pay or receive, from whom/to whom, and how that amount was calculated. The personalized current-user breakdown is implemented, but global group-level suggestions and runtime verification remain outstanding.

## 8. Create a settlement — Not started (API pieces only)

A settlement POST route and service exist, but there is no settlement form. Validation checks basic fields but does not ensure payer/payee membership, different users, or an amount within the outstanding balance.

Plan:

- [ ] Add a form reachable from a suggested balance, prefilled with the payer, payee, outstanding amount, and date.
- [ ] Verify payer and payee are members of the expense and are different people.
- [ ] Prevent settling more than the outstanding balance in that direction; allow partial repayment.
- [ ] Save and display the settlement only after a successful response, with clear failure recovery.
- [ ] Verify balances recalculate immediately after settlement.

Done when a user can record that money was actually paid to another member, with the direction and amount unambiguous.

## 9. View settlements — Not started (API pieces only)

The expense-scoped settlement list API exists, but no settlements UI exists.

Plan:

- [ ] Add a mobile-readable history showing payer, payee, amount, currency, and date.
- [ ] Show an empty state and distinguish recorded repayments from expense payments.
- [ ] Verify partial, multiple, edited, and deleted settlements affect balances and history consistently.

Done when every expense member can review repayments and reconcile them with the remaining balance.

## Additional core work

- [x] Add a member list dialog to the expense title row; the creator can remove members with no active ledger history. (Runtime unverified.)
- [ ] Apply `lib/db/migrations/0002_expense_creator.sql` with `npm run db:migrate` before deploying this change; existing expenses retain unknown creator status.
- [ ] Build the expense detail screen for payment, balance, and settlement flows; it is currently a placeholder.
- [ ] Define safe edit/delete rules for expenses, payments, allocations, and settlements, including the effect on other members' balances and history.
- [x] Define membership removal behavior: only the recorded creator may remove another member, and removal is blocked when active payment, allocation, or settlement history exists. Existing expenses without creator metadata cannot offer removal yet.
- [ ] Add server-side cross-record validation and database constraints for membership, allocation totals, and valid settlement directions.
- [ ] Decide whether trip expenses need receipt/photo attachments, dates, categories, or notes. These help trip tracking but can follow the ledger MVP.
- [x] Keep themed text/background pairs at WCAG AA contrast for normal text in light and dark themes; retain each color's hue while slightly darkening its hover state.
- [ ] Verify accessible mobile layout, touch targets, keyboard behavior, offline/network failure recovery, and currency formatting.
- [ ] Add automated coverage for balance calculations and domain validation, plus an end-to-end smoke path through sign-up, invite join, payment, and settlement when requested/available.

## Review notes

- Statuses above reflect source changes and static review. Account creation, expense creation, list rendering, and database behavior need environment-backed verification.
- User authentication appears to be handled by Neon Auth; the app's own user schema is used for application profiles.
- The TODO describes product behavior agreed in [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md). Update both documents when those rules change.
